NGZnJHakNCUFg1blQzSlk0eW9zeVduV3ZKcEtJd0hpUkxDMVhnRE9rY1lLUzF1eENZaFh0SUNKbmRjanN3YWtZakZud2RYUnlSRkk5b1RZT2VfbFJPXzhZVEVyYnFnRHBwYVlGdFQzaEt2Z2VjaGVXWUE0Qmd5bjZYandmR2M2QXBSY2tKem80OGNlSzh3?oc=5\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">AI stocks slide on fears over infrastructure buildout costs</a></h3><span class=\"source\">Reuters</span></li></ol></section>\n<section><h2>Our view</h2><p class=\"stance\"><span class=\"badge\">neutral</span> Analyst board mixed (3 of 3 views)</p><details><summary>How the analyst board voted</summary><ul class=\"board\"><li><strong>Momentum strategist</strong>: constructive, conviction 4/5. Nifty 50 surged 1.85% with most sectors (Financials, Pharma, FMCG) posting 2% gains, while India VIX stayed flat at a low 17.7, indicating strong domestic momentum and breadth. The rupee strengthened (USD/INR down 0.73%) and US 10‑year yields fell 1.94%, easing financing costs and supporting equity inflows. Despite a broad global equity sell‑off (S&amp;P -2.6%, Nasdaq -3.9%) and heightened US tariff concerns, the domestic rally and sectoral breadth suggest risk appetite remains intact for Indian stocks tomorrow.</li><li><strong>Risk manager</strong>: cautious, conviction 3/5. Global equity markets are sharply down (S&amp;P -2.61%, Nasdaq -3.89%) amid AI sell‑off and new US tariffs, raising spill‑over risk to Indian stocks. A stronger rupee (USD/INR 92.22, -0.73%) and modest Brent rise (+1.07%) benefit import‑heavy sectors but could weigh on exporters, adding currency‑linked downside. Domestic breadth is solid (Financials +2.21%, Pharma +2.38%, FMCG +3.79%) yet India VIX is unchanged at 17.7, indicating limited volatility buffer.</li><li><strong>Macro economist</strong>: constructive, conviction 4/5. Indian equities closed sharply higher (Nifty +1.85%, Sensex +1.82%) with broad sector gains, showing domestic momentum despite a global risk‑off backdrop. The rupee firmed (USD/INR 92.22, -0.73%), easing import costs and supporting foreign‑investor sentiment as the dollar weakens. US equity sell‑off (S&amp;P 500 -2.61%, Nasdaq -3.89%) and falling 10‑year yields signal tighter risk appetite, which could temper the rally but the strong domestic fundamentals and SEBI reforms keep the outlook constructive.</li></ul></details></section>\n",
